# SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH — the Postgres image every CI service container, the
# dev/prod DB image, and the restore drill run.
#
# FORMAT: the first non-blank line that is not a `#` comment is the image
# reference, and nothing else on that line. Consumers parse exactly that.
#
# THE BASE IS STOCK POSTGRES; THE EXTENSIONS ARE INSTALLED ON TOP (#832).
# This used to be `postgis/postgis:16-3.4`, i.e. PostGIS baked into the base.
# That image is Debian bullseye, whose security `Release` file froze on
# 2026-09-07, past which `apt-get update` exits 100 and every build on it
# fails. `postgis/postgis` publishes no Debian tag on a maintained suite for
# Postgres 16 — its `16-3.5` is `FROM postgres:16-bullseye` and reproduces the
# same failure — and its only trixie tag forces a Postgres MAJOR bump. So
# PostGIS and pgvector both come from PGDG now, which changes what the
# consumers below have to agree on: not just this tag, but the package list.
# Trixie's regular security support runs to 2028-08-09.
#
# 2026-09-17 UPDATE — THE ACUTE BREAK HAS LAPSED, THE REASON HAS NOT.
# Debian re-signed bullseye-security on 2026-09-12 09:27:08 UTC, and the
# refreshed InRelease carries NO `Valid-Until:` field at all, so apt cannot
# consider it expired. Verified on the production VM: a `--no-cache` build of
# the OLD Dockerfile, without the flag, exits 0 today. So the three
# `Acquire::Check-Valid-Until=false` flags were load-bearing for five days
# (2026-09-07 to 09-12) and are no-ops now.
#
# That makes this change LESS urgent and NOT less correct. bullseye is
# `oldoldstable`; its LTS ended 2026-08-31. Its index validity now depends on
# someone choosing to re-sign an EOL suite, with no expiry to reason about —
# which is less predictable than a supported suite, not more. A production
# database base should not rest on that.
#
# WHO DERIVES FROM THIS FILE (no literal of their own — they read it):
#   .github/actions/enable-pgvector/action.yml   `docker ps --filter ancestor=`
#
# WHO CANNOT, AND SO REPEATS THE LITERAL:
#   .github/workflows/ci.yml                 (x3, `services.postgres.image`)
#   .github/workflows/lighthouse.yml
#   .github/workflows/coverage-reference.yml
#   .github/workflows/load-test.yml
#   deploy/postgres/Dockerfile               (`FROM`)
#   infra/scripts/restore-test-gcp.sh        (heredoc `FROM`)
#   docs/dev-setup-macos.md                  (arm64 table)
# GitHub Actions gives `jobs.<id>.services.<id>.image` no `env` context and no
# way to call an action, and `FROM` takes no runtime lookup, so those nine
# literals (six `image:` lines, two `FROM`s, one doc table row) are literals
# by necessity, not by choice.
#
# AND TWO MORE SPELLINGS OF IT — so the real count is ELEVEN, not nine.
# `docker-compose.yml` and `docker-compose.test.yml` build
# `deploy/postgres/Dockerfile` and tag the result
# `agri-saas-postgres:<tag>-postgis-pgvector`, where `<tag>` is the tag below
# repeated inside a LOCAL image name. That is the pin again, in a name no
# `postgres:` reference search can find — bump this file and leave those two
# tags behind and they lie about the base, with CI green.
#
# NOTHING ENFORCES ANY OF THIS AUTOMATICALLY. Until 2026-09-17 the paragraph
# here said `tests/guards/postgis-image-single-source.test.ts` "fails CI the
# instant any of them disagrees", and described a "Direction D" that parses
# every compose file. That guard has never existed — verified against
# origin/main with a positive control on the search, twice, by two sessions.
# The claim was worse than the gap: a reader had every reason to believe the
# eleven literals were protected. They are not. Bumping this file means
# grepping for the old tag and changing every site by hand, and the count
# above is prose that can rot like any other. Tracked as #860.
#
# BEFORE YOU BUMP THIS: the package list in `deploy/postgres/Dockerfile` and
# in the `infra/scripts/restore-test-gcp.sh` heredoc must stay in step, and a
# base change that moves glibc requires a REINDEX on every existing cluster —
# see docs/postgis-base-image-options.md §6 and the cutover runbook it names.
postgres:16-trixie
